"""The enforcement half of "third-party bytes never enter Git".

``.gitignore`` is a convenience. It stops an accident that follows the patterns
somebody thought of, it says nothing about a file that was added with ``-f``, and
it has no opinion at all about what is *already* tracked. This module reads what
Git actually tracks and answers the only question that matters for a public
repository: is there an upstream byte in it?

Seven rules, each named in the audit it produces so that a finding says which one
fired and a reader can argue with it:

``model_weight_extension``       a serialized model, by extension
``known_checkpoint_filename``    the filenames checkpoints are published under
``runtime_bundle_or_binary``     a compiled object, library or jar
``source_or_artifact_archive``   an archive, which is how upstream source arrives
``dataset_or_biometric_image``   an image or template of the kind a subject produced
``forbidden_third_party_path``   a directory whose whole purpose is vendoring
``known_upstream_artifact_digest``  the exact bytes this project already pins

plus ``oversized_tracked_file``, which is the backstop for the artifact nobody
predicted: no file this repository legitimately tracks is anywhere near a
megabyte, and every upstream artifact it names is far past one.

There is exactly one exception and it is stated, not implied: the ten synthetic
imaging fixtures under ``tests/fixtures/imaging/``. They are checkerboards,
gradients and ridge-shaped stripes generated by ``generate.py`` in that
directory; no subject produced them, and their golden hashes are what proves the
canonical transform still produces the pixels it produced before. The audit
records the exception by name, so an eleventh file appearing there is a finding
(docs/adr/0083).
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import subprocess
from dataclasses import dataclass
from pathlib import Path, PurePosixPath

from fpbench.core.serialization import stable_hash
from fpbench.core.third_party_errors import RepositoryArtifactError

